// Sigil-Logic/clafer#33: closed integer arithmetic as a reference target.
// `negOne -> (1 - 2)` is the binary operator over two literals in the IR (a
// reference target is parsed above the arithmetic levels, so only the
// parentheses reach them), which Common.getRefIds had no case for, so every
// output mode crashed before any backend ran.  The expression now folds to
// the literal it denotes wherever a reference target is consumed -- the five
// operators `+ - * / %` over two integer literals, bottom-up, so nested and
// negated forms fold too -- and both backends land on the #31 literal
// encodings: Alloy declares the bare literal (`one -1`), Choco emits
// `constant(-1)` on the #18 encoding.  Division and remainder truncate
// toward zero, which is what Alloy 6.2.0 (`div`, `rem`) and chocosolver
// (`div`, `mod`) compute, so the assertions comparing each folded
// declaration with the validator's own evaluation of the same expression
// hold: the gated Choco leg solves them on every run, and the .als was
// executed with `alloy exec` when this model was added (every check UNSAT).
// Facts keep the general rendering of the operators, so the constraint side
// of the corpus is unchanged.  Arithmetic that does not fold -- a clafer
// operand, unary minus over a set expression, real literals, division by
// zero -- is a positioned compile error instead (test/negative/si33-*).

negOne -> (1 - 2)
assert [ negOne = 1 - 2 ]

five -> (2 + 3)
assert [ five = 2 + 3 ]

six -> (2 * 3)
assert [ six = 2 * 3 ]

// a negative dividend: truncation toward zero gives -3 and -1, not the
// floored -4 and 1
truncQuot -> ((-7) / 2)
assert [ truncQuot = (-7) / 2 ]
assert [ truncQuot = -3 ]

truncRem -> ((-7) % 2)
assert [ truncRem = (-7) % 2 ]
assert [ truncRem = -1 ]

// nested and negated forms fold bottom-up: (-1) * (-3)
nested -> ((1 - 2) * (-(2 + 1)))
assert [ nested = 3 ]

// inside a set expression: each operand folds in place, then the set
// operator renders around the literals
inSet -> (1 - 2) ++ (2 + 3)
assert [ inSet = -1 || inSet = 5 ]
